   101	### 2. Register it in the package manifest
   102	
   103	Add the mode to `src/extensions/workflow/package.json` under `pi.extensions`:
   104	
   105	```json
   106	{
   107	  "pi": {
   108	    "extensions": [
   109	      "./modes/prototype.ts",
   110	      "./modes/quick.ts",
   111	      "./modes/rigorous.ts"
   112	    ]
   113	  }
   114	}
   115	```
   116	
   117	That's it. The loader picks it up at boot; the three tools (`start_rigorous_workflow`, `next_rigorous_step`, `end_rigorous_workflow`) and the `/rigorous` command are registered automatically.
   118	
   119	## Config reference
   120	
   121	| Field | Type | Description |
   122	|---|---|---|
   123	| `mode` | `string` | Mode name, echoed in entry payloads and messages. |
   124	| `phases` | `Phase[]` | Ordered phase list. `Phase` is `string` — new phase names are allowed. |
   125	| `phaseArtifacts` | `Partial<Record<Phase, string>>` | The artifact file each phase must produce before advancing. Omit a phase to skip its gate. |
   126	| `prompts` | `Partial<Record<Phase, (ctx) => string>>` | Prompt generator per phase. `ctx = { artifactDir, userPrompt }`. |
   127	| `closeArtifacts` | `string[]` | Files that must exist before `end()` succeeds. Config-owned, no built-in default. |
   128	| `skipRules?` | `{ phase, shouldSkip }[]` | Optional per-phase skip rules. `shouldSkip` is a boolean predicate; when true the engine skips to the next phase. Omit to use the adapter's default (skip `reuse` on empty projects). |
   129	| `statusKey` | `string` | Footer status key. |
   130	| `entryType` | `string` | Persisted entry custom-type, used for session resume. |
   131	| `footerLabel` | `string` | Label shown in the footer (`● label · step/total phase`). |
   132	
   133	### Adapter options
   134	
   135	The second argument to `createWorkflowExtension`:
   136	
   137	| Field | Description |
   138	|---|---|
   139	| `toolNames` | `{ start, next, end }` — the three registered tool names. |
   140	| `toolLabels` | Human-readable labels for the tools. |
   141	| `commandName` | The `/<command>` name users type to kick off the workflow. |
   142	| `commandDescription` | Description shown in the command list. |
   143	
   144	## How transitions work
   145	
   146	Every state-machine method returns a `WorkflowEffect` — a discriminated union the adapter switches on:
   147	
   148	| Effect | Meaning |
   149	|---|---|
   150	| `started` | `start()` succeeded; first phase prompt + entry + footer. |
   151	| `alreadyActive` | `start()` called while a workflow is active. |
   152	| `advanced` | Phase moved forward (optionally `skipped` a phase). |
   153	| `blocked` | Current phase's artifact is missing. |
   154	| `terminalNeedsArtifacts` | At the last phase; a close artifact is missing. |
   155	| `terminalReady` | At the last phase; all close artifacts present — call `end()`. |
   156	| `closed` | `end()` succeeded; workflow finished. |
   157	| `endBlocked` | `end()` called from the wrong phase or with close artifacts missing. |
   158	| `idle` | No active workflow. |
   159	| `noOp` | Auto-advance checked, nothing to do (not active, not armed, artifact not present, or already advancing). |
   160	
   161	The `tool_result` auto-advance hook is one line:
   162	
   163	```typescript
   164	const eff = await sm.onArtifactMaybe(deps);
   165	applyEffect(pi, ctx, config, eff);
   166	```
   167	
   168	The reentrancy guard lives inside `onArtifactMaybe` — concurrent calls return `noOp`, so a double `write` in one turn cannot double-advance the phase.
   169	
   170	## Skip rules
   171	
   172	By default the adapter skips the `reuse` phase when the project has no git history. To override, supply `skipRules` in your config:
   173	
   174	```typescript
   175	skipRules: [
   176	  { phase: "research", shouldSkip: async () => isWellUnderstoodDomain() },
   177	  { phase: "reuse", shouldSkip: async () => isEmptyProject() },
   178	],
   179	```
